I can see the looping animation bug. The issue is in `useCountUp`:

```javascript
const start = useCallback((): void => {
    if (!isAnimating) {
      animate();
    }
  }, [isAnimating, animate]);
```

The `start` function has `isAnimating` in its dependency array. When `isAnimating` changes (from true to false after animation completes), `start` gets a new reference. This causes the `useEffect` in `AnimatedCounter` to re-fire:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
  if (startOn === 'viewport' && isVisible) {
    start();
  }
}, [isVisible, start, startOn]);
```

Since `start` is now a new reference, the effect runs again, and with `isAnimating` now false, it triggers `animate()` once more, creating an infinite loop. The solution is to use a ref to track whether animation has already started, removing the need for `isAnimating` in the dependency array.

Now I'm looking at the latency issue where `avgResponseMs` is showing 0ms. I need to check how the snapshot data stores execution metrics.

The snapshot stores `executionsAvgTimeMs` from the daily aggregate, but the problem is that this is only yesterday's data. Since there were no executions yesterday, it's null and displays as 0ms, even though the all-time average is around 6590ms. The homepage should display the all-time average instead of just the daily value. I added fallback code that uses the all-time average, but when the snapshot exists, it defaults to the daily `executionsAvgTimeMs` which is causing the issue. I see the issue now—the homepage is pulling the daily average execution time from the snapshot, which shows null or 0ms when there were no executions yesterday. I need to fix this by querying the all-time execution average directly in the homepage data fetching instead of relying on the snapshot value. I also need to address the animation loop dependency cycle in `useCountUp` where `start` keeps getting recreated when `isAnimating` changes, causing the animation to restart.

The fix is to use a ref for the `isAnimating` check so the `start` function stays stable and doesn't trigger the effect repeatedly.

